        "content": "Building stone becomes an architectural material when its properties are considered alongside its intended use. An exterior façade faces a different combination of weather and maintenance demands from an interior surface. Iranian research makes this distinction especially tangible: studies examine both stone already exposed on Tehran buildings and samples subjected to controlled weathering in laboratories. These approaches answer complementary questions about performance. [s1][s2]\n\nA comparative study of natural stone claddings in Tehran and Lisbon investigated how a service-life assessment method developed in one city could be applied in another. Its central lesson is contextual: durability estimates cannot simply be transferred between countries as universal material constants. Stone type and exposure conditions influence how a façade deteriorates. For readers encountering polished stone on an Iranian street, the visible finish is therefore only the starting point for understanding the material. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tehran-facades",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What Tehran façades reveal",
        "content": "The Tehran investigation used direct inspections of natural stone claddings adhered to their supporting surfaces. It examined the relationship between deterioration and conditions such as stone type and environmental exposure. Wind, rain, and pollutants emerged as important agents of degradation in the inspected sample. The study consequently treats service life as a result of interacting conditions rather than a fixed promise attached to the word stone. [s1]\n\nThis evidence also has a clear boundary. Findings from adhered cladding in a particular sample do not establish the performance of every Iranian façade, every attachment system, or every stone sold under a similar commercial name. Their value lies in demonstrating how buildings can be studied systematically and why maintenance expectations should be linked to actual exposure. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "travertine-weathering",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Travertine under freezing and salt exposure",
        "content": "Travertine from Gerdoee near Azarshahr in northwestern Iran provides a focused example of laboratory durability research. Researchers compared changes in mechanical properties after repeated freezing and thawing in fresh water and in sodium sulfate solution. The salt-bearing exposure produced more severe deterioration in the tested material. The study supports a specific conclusion: the surrounding solution can change the damage produced by a freezing regime. It does not establish a universal ranking of all Iranian travertines. [s2]\n\nA broader review of building-stone research by Iranian investigators likewise identifies freezing and thawing as an important deterioration mechanism in cold-region exterior applications. It examines the diversity of laboratory procedures used to assess that damage. Differences in exposure conditions and testing methods matter when interpreting apparently comparable durability claims. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-performance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading durability claims carefully",
        "content": "Together, field inspection and laboratory testing offer a more useful account of building stone than appearance alone. Field studies show deterioration under real exposure; laboratory studies isolate mechanisms under defined conditions. Neither automatically supplies a guaranteed lifespan for an individual building. [s1][s2]\n\nFor an overview of Iran's building-stone sector, these studies establish an important technical foundation. A fuller account still requires verified evidence on quarry-to-factory processing, regional supply chains, façade attachment practices, and comparable production statistics. Those subjects remain research gaps here rather than assumptions filled with industry rankings or unsupported market totals.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
