    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Building insulation slows heat transfer through the building envelope, helping maintain indoor comfort. Iranian research illustrates how material choice interacts with wall construction, orientation, climate, and patterns of building use.",
    "tags": ["Insulation", "Building envelope", "Energy efficiency", "Thermal comfort", "Iranian architecture"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:15:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:15:48Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Building Insulation in Iran: Materials, Climate and Performance",
      "description": "Understand insulation materials, thermal bridges, and Iranian building simulations without confusing modeled savings with measured national outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "heat-and-envelope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What insulation changes",
        "content": "Insulation slows the movement of heat through the building envelope: the walls, roof, and floor separating indoor space from outdoor conditions. It can help retain heat in winter and limit heat entering in summer. Materials work by mechanisms including trapping relatively still air and reflecting radiant heat. Their usefulness depends on the construction in which they are installed. [s1]\n\nA common measure is thermal resistance, or R-value. A higher R-value indicates greater resistance to heat flow, but the product rating does not describe every pathway through a finished building. Thermal bridges allow heat to bypass insulation through other components. The performance of the completed envelope therefore depends on continuity as well as the properties of the insulating material. Climate and construction type influence the appropriate approach. These are general building-physics principles; the Australian guidance cited here does not establish Iranian regulatory requirements. [s1]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tehran-materials",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a Tehran simulation can establish",
        "content": "A study published in the Journal of Thermal Engineering investigated wall insulation in Tehran using DesignBuilder, software for modeling building energy performance. It compared configurations that varied insulation type and thickness. The abstract identifies polyurethane, fiberglass board, and mineral wool among materials that improved the modeled thermal performance. This provides evidence about the studied configurations, rather than a measurement of savings across Tehran's housing stock. [s2]\n\nThe distinction matters when reading claims about an optimal thickness or a best material. A simulation evaluates a defined building and a specified set of assumptions. Its result is relevant to those conditions; applying it elsewhere requires examining how closely the other building matches them. The authors themselves frame the work around Tehran's climate and local construction practices, while identifying wider application as a direction for further research. [s2]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "orientation-and-occupation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Orientation and the way rooms are used",
        "content": "A master's thesis held by the University of Genoa examined Iranian building cases through simulations of insulation and other interventions. Its repository abstract reports that orientation changed the relationship between insulation thickness and heating and cooling savings. In the studied comparison, the north-facing case required more insulation to achieve positive savings than the northeast-facing case, despite consuming less thermal energy initially. This is a case-specific finding, not a general prescription for north-facing Iranian buildings. [s3]\n\nThe thesis also examined a hot, dry-desert case in which occupants used different rooms seasonally. Within that analysis, seasonal room use performed favorably against the alternative involving heating, ventilation, and air-conditioning systems. The finding draws attention to occupancy and passive architectural strategies alongside added insulation. It does not demonstrate that traditional buildings always outperform contemporary buildings. [s3]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-results",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A material within a larger system",
        "content": "The practical lesson is to understand insulation as part of an envelope and a pattern of use. Material resistance, continuity, climate, and building configuration all affect the result. Moisture management also belongs in the assessment because insulation selection and placement interact with condensation risk. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nFor Iran, the inspected research offers useful examples of how those relationships can be investigated. It does not establish national installation rates, verified compliance levels, or measured annual savings. Keeping those questions separate allows technical research to inform the discussion without turning a modeled case into a claim about an entire country."
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
